---
// /compare — honest alternatives. Positioning term (tests/positioning.spec.ts):
// "alternatives", in the title, the description and the H1.
// Rule (CONTENT_GUIDE.md): comparisons are factual, sourced and dated; never "the
// only". The statements about other products live in src/data/compare.ts.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
import { GROUPS, CHECKED, MESSAGE_FIRST, FIRST_CHECKED, GUIDE, CLAUDE_CODE, CLAUDE_CODE_CHECKED, RUNS, RUN_ROWS, RUNS_MEASURED, RUNS_PAGESPEED } from '../data/compare';
---
<Base
  title="Which website tool fits? The alternatives"
  description="What are you building? Croftweaver and its alternatives: WordPress, Joomla, Drupal, hosted builders, AI app builders, AI assistant skills, and when each fits."
  image="/images/og/compare.jpg"
>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <h1>Which website tool fits what you are building?</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Croftweaver and its alternatives, side by side. For many people one of the
      alternatives is the better choice, and this page says when.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      In the groups below, every statement about another product restates what its
      own page says and links to it. Those links were checked on
      <time datetime={CHECKED.iso}>{CHECKED.label}</time>, unless a group gives its own date.
      Prices and plans change.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href={SITE.repo}>Get Croftweaver on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="short">
    <h2>What are you building?</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      {GUIDE.map((g) => (
        <li><strong>{g.building}:</strong> <a href={g.href}>{g.choose}</a>, {g.why}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Each line points to a section below, where the products are named and linked.</p>
    <p>
      Already on WordPress and thinking of leaving? See <a href="/from-wordpress">From WordPress to Astro</a>,
      and <a href="/security">what a static website changes for security</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="claude-code">
    <h2>Claude Code on its own, or with Croftweaver</h2>
    <p>
      Croftweaver does not replace Claude Code. It is a set of skills for AI coding assistants,
      developed and used with Claude Code, with install guides for Codex and Antigravity too.
      Claude Code is <q>{CLAUDE_CODE.what}</q>, and skills are how it packages
      <q>{CLAUDE_CODE.skills}</q> (<a href={CLAUDE_CODE.url}>{CLAUDE_CODE.label}</a>, read on
      <time datetime={CLAUDE_CODE_CHECKED.iso}>{CLAUDE_CODE_CHECKED.label}</time>). Croftweaver is a
      set of such skills, for websites. What they bring:
    </p>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li><strong>Positioning before copy.</strong> The assistant works out with you what the site has to say before it writes a page. <a href="/positioning">How positioning works</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>The site's own test suites.</strong> By default they run before each push and on each pull request. <a href="/checks">The checks</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Search and AI basics from the first build.</strong> Titles and descriptions within limits, structured data, an llms.txt file and a share image for each page, kept in place by those tests. <a href="/ai-website-seo-checklist">The SEO checklist</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>After launch, if you connect them, the next edit from real data.</strong> Search Console insights, and an optional weekly check of whether AI assistants name the business, run with your own API keys. <a href="/established-sites">Established sites</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Use Claude Code on its own when</strong> you want a one-off page or a prototype, or you
      already have your own method and checks. <strong>Use it with Croftweaver when</strong> you want
      this method, and by default the same checks on every change.
    </p>
    <h3 id="one-comparison">One comparison, side by side</h3>
    <p>
      The same brief for a fictional business went to Claude Code twice, with the same model and the
      same settings: once on its own, once with Croftweaver. Each run started from a one-line prompt,
      quoted in its repository; neither stopped to ask a question, and neither was helped along. The
      experimenter deployed both sites the same way and measured them with tools Croftweaver does not
      make. It is a test of each
      set-up's defaults on the same brief, not of an agreed checklist both had to meet. One run each is
      an anecdote, not a measurement: it cannot show that one way builds better sites in general, how
      either site would rank, or whether AI assistants would cite it, and positioning, the quality of
      the copy, Croftweaver's own publishing steps and later changes were not compared.
    </p>
    <div id="runs-comparison" class="table-wrap stack" role="region" aria-label="One comparison: Claude Code on its own and with Croftweaver" tabindex="0">
      <table role="table">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Measure</th>
            {RUNS.map((r) => <th scope="col" role="columnheader">{r.name}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          {RUN_ROWS.map((row) => (
            <tr role="row">
              <th scope="row" role="rowheader">{row.measure}</th>
              {RUNS.map((r) => <td role="cell" data-label={r.name}>{row[r.key]}</td>)}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Measured on <time datetime={RUNS_MEASURED.iso}>{RUNS_MEASURED.label}</time>. The cost is the
      figure Claude Code reported for the run. HTML errors and warnings: the W3C Nu HTML Checker;
      structured data: Google's Rich Results Test; both on the home and contact pages. PageSpeed:
      PageSpeed Insights on mobile, the median of five runs, for performance, accessibility, best
      practices and SEO, on <time datetime={RUNS_PAGESPEED.iso}>{RUNS_PAGESPEED.label}</time>. Both
      sites fail one SEO item, the noindex both carry; 66 against 69 comes from one item that does not
      apply to the site without images. The sites:
      {RUNS.map((r, i) => <>{i > 0 && ' and '}<a href={r.site}>{r.siteLabel}</a></>)}.
      Each repository holds the brief, the prompt, the run's history, its transcript in plain text
      (tool results shortened) and the measurements:
      {RUNS.map((r, i) => <>{i > 0 && ' and '}<a href={r.repo}>{r.repoLabel}</a></>)}.
      The experimenter added a header to both sites asking search engines not to index them, because
      the business is fictional.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="first">
    <h2>Croftweaver starts with your positioning and keeps your pages on it</h2>
    <p>
      A website has to convince someone. So before Croftweaver writes a line of copy
      or picks a colour, your assistant works out with you what the site has to say:
      what you offer, for whom, what they would use instead, and what you have that
      those alternatives do not. For what you claim, it asks what shows that it is
      true: a figure, the way it works, a reference. Quotes from your customers, where you
      have them, go in with the name of the person and the thing they reviewed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The answers go into one file in your repository, and that is where the work
      begins rather than ends. Each content page gets the term it owns, and from then
      on a test checks that it still carries that term in its title, its description
      and its opening. A change that drops it fails that test, which by default runs
      before every push and on every pull request. A page you have not given a term yet
      only raises a warning, and legal pages need none. This is a
      method, not a result: whether the positioning is right stays your judgment.
      <a href="/positioning">How positioning works</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Starting with the message is not Croftweaver's alone. Among the
      <a href="#skills">skills for AI coding assistants</a> below,
      {MESSAGE_FIRST.map((m, i) => (
        <>{i === 0 ? ' ' : i === MESSAGE_FIRST.length - 1 ? '; and ' : '; '}<a href={m.url}>{i === 0 ? m.name : m.name.replace(/^The /, 'the ')}</a> {m.fact}</>
      ))}.
      Read from each project's own page on
      <time datetime={FIRST_CHECKED.iso}>{FIRST_CHECKED.label}</time>.
    </p>
  </section>

  {GROUPS.map((group) => (
    <section class="wrap" id={group.id}>
      <h2>{group.title}</h2>
      <p>{group.what}</p>
      <ul class="examples" role="list">
        {group.examples.map((e) => (
          <li><a href={e.url}>{e.name}</a><span>{e.fact}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      {'needs' in group && (
        <p class="note needs">
          {group.needs.text} Sources:{' '}
          {group.needs.sources.map((src, i) => (
            <>{i > 0 && ', '}<a href={src.url}>{src.label}</a></>
          ))}. The links in this group were read on <time datetime={group.checked.iso}>{group.checked.label}</time>.
        </p>
      )}
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Choose one of these when</h3>
          <p>{group.chooseThem}{'themMore' in group && <> {group.themMore.lead} <a href={group.themMore.href}>{group.themMore.label}</a>.</>}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Choose Croftweaver when</h3>
          <p>{group.chooseCroftweaver}{'more' in group && <> <a href={group.more.href}>{group.more.label}</a>.</>}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      {'together' in group && (
        <p class="note"><strong>Use them together.</strong> {group.together.text} <a href={group.together.url}>{group.together.name}</a>.</p>
      )}
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="wrap" id="wrong-choice">
    <h2>When Croftweaver is the wrong choice</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li><strong>You want to see and drag things.</strong> Croftweaver has no visual editor.</li>
      <li><strong>You need a shop, accounts or a database.</strong> It builds content sites.</li>
      <li><strong>You want someone else to host and run it.</strong> You publish to hosting you control.</li>
      <li><strong>You do not want an AI coding assistant.</strong> Croftweaver needs one, and building a new site with it needs a GitHub account too.</li>
      <li><strong>You want proof from many sites.</strong> <a href="/proof">The proof page</a> shows one, and <a href="/case-study">the case study</a> follows this site week by week.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      What is missing and what comes next is on <a href="/roadmap">the roadmap</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href={SITE.repo}>Get Croftweaver on GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/start">Quickstart</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section.wrap { padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem; max-width: var(--maxw); margin: 0 auto; }
  section.hero { padding-top: 3rem; }
  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 1rem; hyphens: manual; }
  h2 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  section p, .steps { max-width: 46rem; line-height: 1.7; hyphens: manual; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .note { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

  /* role="list" in the markup: Safari drops list semantics when list-style is none. */
  .examples { list-style: none; margin: 1rem 0 0; padding: 0; max-width: 56rem; }
  .examples li {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 12rem) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0.2rem 1.5rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .examples a { font-weight: 600; }
  .examples span { color: var(--muted); }
  .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 0; max-width: 56rem; }
  .card p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.6; }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .examples li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
